1
00:00:06,133 --> 00:00:08,533
[intense music playing]

2
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:12,700
[Jerry] This is a 1959 Porsche RSK Spyder.

3
00:00:15,066 --> 00:00:18,900
It has a four cylinder,
1.6 liter flat four engine.

4
00:00:19,766 --> 00:00:21,833
It’s the 19th one ever made.

5
00:00:22,533 --> 00:00:24,266
It is not a street car.

6
00:00:24,533 --> 00:00:26,099
It is not for highways.

7
00:00:27,133 --> 00:00:29,133
It is not for neighborhoods.

8
00:00:29,733 --> 00:00:31,700
It is not for casual driving.

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,000
This...

10
00:00:36,266 --> 00:00:37,699
is a race car.

11
00:00:37,833 --> 00:00:40,066
[engine revving]

12
00:00:46,466 --> 00:00:48,466
[Jerry] This was the car
that Porsche used to prove

13
00:00:48,533 --> 00:00:50,199
that a small car with a small engine

14
00:00:50,266 --> 00:00:53,099
could be faster than a big car
with a big engine.

15
00:00:55,666 --> 00:00:58,033
If you were a racer in the '50s,
and you rolled

16
00:00:58,099 --> 00:01:00,300
one of these little aluminum beauties
out onto the track,

17
00:01:01,199 --> 00:01:03,566
you knew you had a chance
to beat anything.

18
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:10,100
Because it was built
like a small airplane.

19
00:01:10,166 --> 00:01:13,933
It has a mid-engine design,
aluminum body and four cam engine.

20
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:15,600
[engine revving]

21
00:01:15,733 --> 00:01:18,133
This car was designed
a half a century ago.

22
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:20,866
Somehow, it still looks
like it’s coming out next year.

23
00:01:21,399 --> 00:01:23,000
I don’t know why it sounds so good,

24
00:01:24,666 --> 00:01:26,433
I don’t know why it’s so beautiful.

25
00:01:27,266 --> 00:01:30,266
But if you just want to go for a drive
for the pure joy of it,

26
00:01:31,266 --> 00:01:33,266
I think it’s just about the best there is.

27
00:01:33,566 --> 00:01:36,133
[upbeat music playing]

28
00:01:42,899 --> 00:01:45,100
[slow music playing]

29
00:01:48,233 --> 00:01:51,866
Because of its economy of dimension
and tremendous performance,

30
00:01:52,166 --> 00:01:55,366
I felt like this was the perfect car
for my very special guest this week,

31
00:01:55,433 --> 00:01:56,699
Mr. Kevin Hart.

32
00:01:56,766 --> 00:01:57,933
Yeah!

33
00:01:58,866 --> 00:02:01,199
[Jerry] Kevin doesn’t take up
a lot of space either,

34
00:02:01,266 --> 00:02:04,000
but he is one of the most fun guys
in the world.

35
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:10,166
Hi, I’m Jerry Seinfeld, and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

36
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:19,866
-You’re out of Philly?
-I am out of Philadelphia.

37
00:02:19,933 --> 00:02:23,366
It’s a town where if somebody goes,
"Hey man, you got talent,"

38
00:02:23,433 --> 00:02:25,466
if somebody in Philadelphia says that,

39
00:02:25,533 --> 00:02:26,633
-you’re gonna make it.
-Right.

40
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:27,966
-Philadelphia’s tough.
-Yeah.

41
00:02:28,033 --> 00:02:31,000
I saw a dog get booed at halftime
at a Eagles' game

42
00:02:31,066 --> 00:02:32,433
‘cause he dropped the Frisbee.

43
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:34,100
Man, like, literally.

44
00:02:34,233 --> 00:02:35,700
Boo!

45
00:02:36,866 --> 00:02:39,133
[Kevin] Okay, two questions.
One, what is it?

46
00:02:39,233 --> 00:02:41,366
-It’s a Porsche Spyder.
-It’s a Porsche Spyder, okay.

47
00:02:41,433 --> 00:02:43,000
This is the Porsche philosophy.

48
00:02:43,299 --> 00:02:47,033
Smaller, smarter, better.
This is how I see you.

49
00:02:47,333 --> 00:02:48,866
-Smaller, better.
-Better.

50
00:02:49,033 --> 00:02:50,533
[both laughing]

51
00:02:52,266 --> 00:02:53,633
Uh, key question.

52
00:02:54,299 --> 00:02:55,299
Is it safe?

53
00:02:55,366 --> 00:02:56,366
No.

54
00:03:03,366 --> 00:03:04,666
Oh, Jerry. This is nice.

55
00:03:04,733 --> 00:03:06,799
-What do you think of this?
-No, this is nice.

56
00:03:07,033 --> 00:03:09,833
[engine whirring]

57
00:03:12,633 --> 00:03:14,933
-The car is very loud!
-Yes.

58
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,299
This isn’t a good date car.
Like your first time with a lady.

59
00:03:18,366 --> 00:03:22,166
[shouting] "You look very beautiful.
You look amazing in the wind."

60
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:31,633
[Kevin] New York is the only place
I’ve ever been hit by a car.

61
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:34,133
I got hit by a car, and I was so shocked,

62
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:36,066
I got back up and just started walking.

63
00:03:36,133 --> 00:03:38,933
Here’s the part that you’re going to think
I’m making up a joke,

64
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:40,000
-but I’m not.
-Yeah.

65
00:03:40,066 --> 00:03:42,366
I had a bag of chicken in my hand.

66
00:03:42,433 --> 00:03:44,799
The car hit me,
I never let go of the chicken.

67
00:03:45,066 --> 00:03:46,333
I never... Listen,

68
00:03:46,399 --> 00:03:48,533
I remember getting up, walking,

69
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:50,933
and like a block down,
I just started eating the chicken.

70
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,566
I called my lady,

71
00:03:53,099 --> 00:03:55,001
I said, "Babe, I just got hit by a car."

72
00:03:55,066 --> 00:03:57,366
-"But I got the chicken."
-"I got the chicken, though."

73
00:03:57,433 --> 00:03:59,100
So were you hurt at all, after?

74
00:03:59,166 --> 00:04:01,333
Yes. Yes, yes I was.

75
00:04:01,799 --> 00:04:04,066
Very uncomfortable moment
where you go to the doctor,

76
00:04:04,133 --> 00:04:06,833
and he says, "What happened?"
And you say, "I got hit by a car."

77
00:04:06,899 --> 00:04:09,133
And he says, "When?"
And you go, "Two weeks ago."

78
00:04:13,266 --> 00:04:15,600
[Kevin] I love just the driving
and looking around.

79
00:04:15,966 --> 00:04:17,233
I haven’t done this in a while.

80
00:04:20,533 --> 00:04:24,966
I’m afraid of becoming the guy
that expects famous stuff all the time.

81
00:04:25,033 --> 00:04:26,966
-Really?
-I like to do stuff myself.

82
00:04:27,033 --> 00:04:31,000
People don’t realize, to be a comedian,
you gotta be a guy who can do anything.

83
00:04:31,099 --> 00:04:33,166
Anything. Don’t do nothing for me.

84
00:04:33,266 --> 00:04:36,599
"Mr. Hart, let me take your coat."
"Why? It’s my coat."

85
00:04:36,733 --> 00:04:40,099
[upbeat music playing]

86
00:04:42,366 --> 00:04:44,833
I’m still laughing
that you hung on to the chicken.

87
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:47,000
I held the bag in the air,
like... [exclaims]

88
00:04:49,066 --> 00:04:51,300
They had just came out
with the little drummets,

89
00:04:51,357 --> 00:04:52,358
and we saw the commercial.

90
00:04:52,733 --> 00:04:56,099
For anybody that thinks
the KFC promos don’t work, they’re wrong.

91
00:04:56,166 --> 00:04:58,833
We saw the promo,
and I was like, "Oh, [beep].

92
00:04:59,533 --> 00:05:00,599
We got to get that."

93
00:05:00,666 --> 00:05:02,966
[Jerry] That is one of the most
fun things in life,

94
00:05:03,033 --> 00:05:04,700
you see something on TV,

95
00:05:04,766 --> 00:05:06,266
"I’m going to get it right now."

96
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:10,833
-I like... I like that advertising works.
-[laughing]

97
00:05:11,233 --> 00:05:13,599
I couldn’t even continue
watching the show.

98
00:05:17,533 --> 00:05:19,400
-How many kids you got, two?
-[Kevin] Two kids.

99
00:05:19,466 --> 00:05:21,300
And my daughter is nine, my son is six.

100
00:05:21,366 --> 00:05:24,000
-So I found it was like second grade...
-Okay.

101
00:05:24,066 --> 00:05:25,533
...when they came home, and they said,

102
00:05:25,599 --> 00:05:28,000
"Dad, you know,
everybody knows who you are."

103
00:05:28,066 --> 00:05:29,900
My daughter got right to the point,

104
00:05:29,966 --> 00:05:31,133
"Dad, are we rich?

105
00:05:32,266 --> 00:05:33,733
Are we rich, yes or no?"

106
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:37,433
Then I say, "Baby, we’re doing well,
but understand why."

107
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:40,333
I said, "When you work hard,
you put your mind to something,

108
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:42,900
anything can happen,
and that’s what I want you to do."

109
00:05:42,966 --> 00:05:44,933
My daughter’s so smart, she goes,

110
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,833
"Just answer my question, are we rich?"

111
00:05:47,900 --> 00:05:49,333
You know what I say to that question?

112
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:51,000
-"I am."
-"I am." Yeah.

113
00:05:51,066 --> 00:05:52,066
"You’re not."

114
00:05:55,866 --> 00:05:58,633
[Kevin] You know what made me a man?
Public transportation.

115
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:00,333
You want to toughen a kid up,

116
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:03,266
throw a kid on the bus
with the most random group of people

117
00:06:03,333 --> 00:06:04,933
that he’ll ever see in his life.

118
00:06:04,997 --> 00:06:10,370
Also, that at any given time
in my neighborhood, you could die.

119
00:06:10,436 --> 00:06:11,437
I had that neighborhood.

120
00:06:11,504 --> 00:06:13,473
-Really? That bad?
-That was a bad neighborhood.

121
00:06:13,566 --> 00:06:14,566
Bad.

122
00:06:15,633 --> 00:06:16,966
[Kevin] Know what we should do?

123
00:06:17,033 --> 00:06:19,900
We should try to stay as close
to the trash truck as possible.

124
00:06:20,146 --> 00:06:21,881
Don’t worry about it.

125
00:06:21,966 --> 00:06:24,166
Because this smell is not bad at all.

126
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,466
The thing that made me into who I am,

127
00:06:28,533 --> 00:06:30,200
my kids will never experience.

128
00:06:30,266 --> 00:06:31,299
-That’s right.
-Like...

129
00:06:31,366 --> 00:06:32,933
It’s going to be different for them.

130
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,066
Your problem was, "Things are bad,
I got to make it good."

131
00:06:36,133 --> 00:06:37,733
-Yes.
-Their problem is going to be,

132
00:06:37,799 --> 00:06:39,933
"Things are good, why do I feel bad?"

133
00:06:40,466 --> 00:06:41,466
Well, I need it,

134
00:06:42,133 --> 00:06:44,799
and he was like, "I’m back."
And I was like, "Well, I don’t..."

135
00:06:44,866 --> 00:06:46,166
Excuse me, is this a Ferrari?

136
00:06:46,666 --> 00:06:48,599
-A Porsche. Yes.
-Porsche!

137
00:06:48,641 --> 00:06:49,475
Nice Porsche.

138
00:06:51,799 --> 00:06:54,333
Here’s the thing about black people.

139
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:56,933
We don’t care about interrupting anything.

140
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,500
That is not true about black people.

141
00:06:59,566 --> 00:07:00,633
-That’s people.
-No.

142
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:03,433
It’s mainly my people.
We will interrupt anything.

143
00:07:03,489 --> 00:07:07,927
"Sir, we’re talking, give us--"
"Wait a minute. What is this?"

144
00:07:08,261 --> 00:07:11,764
It’s what we do. It’s our nature.
I’ve been guilty of it several times.

145
00:07:11,866 --> 00:07:13,133
-Is that right?
-Yes.

146
00:07:15,599 --> 00:07:16,633
Are you a movie guy?

147
00:07:16,733 --> 00:07:17,966
I go to every movie.

148
00:07:18,033 --> 00:07:19,900
-I’m a movie buff.
-But I don’t have On Demand.

149
00:07:19,966 --> 00:07:21,433
We have a Jewish On Demand.

150
00:07:21,766 --> 00:07:24,133
-And what is that? What?
-"When you get a chance."

151
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:25,266
I say to the TV,

152
00:07:25,333 --> 00:07:27,299
-"Whenever you get a chance."
-I get it now.

153
00:07:31,299 --> 00:07:33,233
See, there’s another black man
interrupting us.

154
00:07:33,299 --> 00:07:34,799
[Kevin] Know what, it’s not just us.

155
00:07:34,866 --> 00:07:36,099
How you doing, sir?

156
00:07:36,166 --> 00:07:38,400
I don’t like people
who carry plastic bags.

157
00:07:38,466 --> 00:07:40,866
It looks like you don’t want
to win in life.

158
00:07:40,933 --> 00:07:42,866
You know what’s the equivalent
of a plastic bag?

159
00:07:42,933 --> 00:07:44,466
-What?
-A neck tattoo.

160
00:07:44,533 --> 00:07:48,599
A neck tattoo says, "I gave up,
think whatever you want to think."

161
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:51,400
How do you go
to your parent teacher conference

162
00:07:51,466 --> 00:07:53,933
with the neck tattoo that says,
"[bleep] life?"

163
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,333
And how are you talking about your kid

164
00:07:56,400 --> 00:07:59,266
not doing what he’s supposed to?
"Well, I gotta talk to him."

165
00:07:59,333 --> 00:08:03,333
"Well, sir, maybe we should have
somebody talk to you both."

166
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:05,833
[upbeat music playing]

167
00:08:08,888 --> 00:08:10,857
I’m gonna go on record saying this:

168
00:08:11,357 --> 00:08:13,392
-you are a good driver.
-Thank you.

169
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:18,933
I have a couple of souped up cars.

170
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:20,099
Oh, really?

171
00:08:20,166 --> 00:08:21,633
I have a Ferrari.

172
00:08:21,700 --> 00:08:24,666
So, in the Ferrari, there's a race button.

173
00:08:25,066 --> 00:08:28,666
You press that button,
you are controlling the car

174
00:08:28,733 --> 00:08:30,466
a hundred percent.

175
00:08:31,233 --> 00:08:32,866
When you don't push this button,

176
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:34,699
when you driving regular,

177
00:08:35,133 --> 00:08:39,400
it's computer generated,
so the car is helping you.

178
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:41,600
-So when you go fast at a turn...
-I understand.

179
00:08:41,666 --> 00:08:44,266
...it's not turning the way
that it would turn

180
00:08:44,333 --> 00:08:45,766
as if you didn't have control.

181
00:08:45,833 --> 00:08:46,966
-I understand.
-Okay.

182
00:08:47,033 --> 00:08:49,333
You can stop the explanation
at this point.

183
00:08:49,395 --> 00:08:52,064
-I was talking really slow, wasn't I?
-You were explaining it to me

184
00:08:52,133 --> 00:08:53,333
-like I was your mother.
-Okay.

185
00:08:55,666 --> 00:08:58,133
[Kevin] I am a very nice guy
to the homeless.

186
00:08:58,199 --> 00:09:01,366
I think I do too much and I think
I’m helping and sometimes I’m not.

187
00:09:01,433 --> 00:09:02,666
I bought one guy a tent.

188
00:09:02,933 --> 00:09:05,933
It had two little lounge chairs
that you could sit inside.

189
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:08,033
It was too much. If you’re homeless,

190
00:09:08,766 --> 00:09:11,066
nine times out of ten,
you’re not good with directions.

191
00:09:11,133 --> 00:09:14,100
I want to commend you for your generosity

192
00:09:14,166 --> 00:09:17,533
in saying nine out of ten.
Because it’s ten out of ten.

193
00:09:17,590 --> 00:09:20,426
[Kevin] My fear was, I hope
the other homeless don’t get jealous,

194
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:22,199
like, "Look at Gary there with the tent."

195
00:09:22,261 --> 00:09:23,829
They might throw him out for technically

196
00:09:23,896 --> 00:09:25,798
-not being homeless at that moment.
-Being homeless.

197
00:09:25,865 --> 00:09:27,600
"It looks like you got a home here, Phil.

198
00:09:27,666 --> 00:09:30,033
I see a door and a window.

199
00:09:30,100 --> 00:09:31,800
And two chairs."

200
00:09:31,866 --> 00:09:35,366
"The guys have been talking, Phil,
and nobody’s happy about this tent."

201
00:09:35,433 --> 00:09:38,566
"I think Phil, you gotta ask yourself,
'Are we homeless or not?'

202
00:09:38,633 --> 00:09:40,699
If you’re not, you gotta go."

203
00:09:44,784 --> 00:09:46,352
[inaudible]

204
00:09:52,433 --> 00:09:53,600
Look at this bike.

205
00:09:53,966 --> 00:09:55,199
That’s kind of cool, huh?

206
00:09:55,266 --> 00:09:57,133
You have a problem. You have a problem.

207
00:09:57,196 --> 00:09:59,966
This right here’s you. "What is this?

208
00:10:00,399 --> 00:10:03,002
-This has gotta be something."
-[cackling]

209
00:10:10,866 --> 00:10:12,366
[Kevin] Just gonna have some coffee.

210
00:10:12,433 --> 00:10:13,500
Two iced coffees.

211
00:10:18,699 --> 00:10:20,199
I really can’t take this heat.

212
00:10:20,266 --> 00:10:22,066
Well, you put the blazer on, Jerry.

213
00:10:22,133 --> 00:10:24,666
-You don’t care.
-I really... I don’t, Jerry.

214
00:10:24,733 --> 00:10:26,933
I can give a damn
about your jacket, Jerry.

215
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,900
You use my name
like you’re trying to sell me something.

216
00:10:29,966 --> 00:10:31,366
-Jerry.
-Yeah. All of a sudden,

217
00:10:31,433 --> 00:10:32,900
you feel like, "Am I buying an Audi?"

218
00:10:32,966 --> 00:10:34,199
Here’s the thing, Jerry.

219
00:10:34,266 --> 00:10:36,900
What you got to understand is
you got two different types of people.

220
00:10:36,966 --> 00:10:38,100
Can we agree on that, Jerry?

221
00:10:38,166 --> 00:10:41,400
Have you talked with, like, executives
that will use your name a lot?

222
00:10:41,466 --> 00:10:42,466
This is what I get now.

223
00:10:42,533 --> 00:10:46,366
"Kevin, what you are doing
is unbelievable.

224
00:10:46,433 --> 00:10:48,533
We need a Kevin here."

225
00:10:48,633 --> 00:10:50,566
And they draw stuff that makes no sense.

226
00:10:50,633 --> 00:10:52,233
"How do we...

227
00:10:52,300 --> 00:10:54,166
get this...

228
00:10:54,233 --> 00:10:56,900
inside of this."

229
00:10:57,433 --> 00:11:00,333
"I focused on the wrong things.
What did you just draw?"

230
00:11:06,466 --> 00:11:09,000
Thank you.
This isn’t what I thought it would be.

231
00:11:09,068 --> 00:11:11,270
-Is this what you thought it would be?
-It’s plastic.

232
00:11:11,366 --> 00:11:12,699
I don’t really mind the plastic.

233
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:17,166
That is hilarious to me. That’s... Yep.

234
00:11:17,233 --> 00:11:18,100
Thank you.

235
00:11:18,166 --> 00:11:20,800
Everything so far is wrong.

236
00:11:20,866 --> 00:11:23,133
It’s hot, there’s no AC.

237
00:11:23,199 --> 00:11:27,033
It’s cold, but it’s not in glass,
which would keep it cold.

238
00:11:27,100 --> 00:11:29,166
And I have more cream than coffee.

239
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:31,833
Oh, my God. Nailed that.

240
00:11:33,366 --> 00:11:34,366
Nailed it.

241
00:11:37,433 --> 00:11:40,133
There’s a guy to your left
who’s on his laptop,

242
00:11:40,199 --> 00:11:43,733
he’s on the phone,
he’s going about his business on our show.

243
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:47,166
[Kevin] I love the people that turn
these coffee shops into their office.

244
00:11:47,233 --> 00:11:49,466
"I bought a cup of coffee,
I live here now."

245
00:11:54,033 --> 00:11:55,900
[Jerry] I’ve never seen anybody
faster than you.

246
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,366
You get into a bit faster
than anyone I’ve ever seen.

247
00:11:59,433 --> 00:12:02,166
I don’t just want it to be a comedy show,
I want it to be an event.

248
00:12:03,433 --> 00:12:05,466
That’s where the fire came in,

249
00:12:05,533 --> 00:12:07,866
before I get to the stage
to tell my jokes.

250
00:12:08,300 --> 00:12:09,733
First of all, I don’t like

251
00:12:10,766 --> 00:12:13,066
hyping up a show
before a guy comes out to talk

252
00:12:13,133 --> 00:12:15,033
because it makes the talking seem small.

253
00:12:15,100 --> 00:12:16,366
-Mmm-hmm.
-That’s my opinion.

254
00:12:16,733 --> 00:12:18,266
-I love these talks, by the way.
-Yeah.

255
00:12:18,333 --> 00:12:20,733
My aesthetic role model is Mike Tyson.

256
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:24,266
Do you remember his robe?
He would cut the hole in the hotel towel,

257
00:12:24,310 --> 00:12:26,979
-no socks, no robe. No stool.
-That’s how he came into the ring.

258
00:12:27,066 --> 00:12:28,066
He came ready to fight.

259
00:12:28,133 --> 00:12:30,000
It’s a reduced essence.

260
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:33,400
Good stand-up is always
a reduced essence of thought.

261
00:12:33,466 --> 00:12:34,866
But here’s what I do like,

262
00:12:34,933 --> 00:12:38,833
I would want visual context
with certain things that you talk about.

263
00:12:39,199 --> 00:12:40,566
-Show me.
-Wow.

264
00:12:40,633 --> 00:12:41,966
-On that screen.
-On the screen.

265
00:12:42,033 --> 00:12:44,166
That I think could be interesting.

266
00:12:44,233 --> 00:12:46,466
-Wow.
-That’s yours.

267
00:12:46,533 --> 00:12:48,966
First of all, I hate that you did this.

268
00:12:54,240 --> 00:12:57,309
I have an idea of what I want
to talk about basically in my phone,

269
00:12:57,376 --> 00:13:01,147
and what I’ll do is I go on
and I talk about the stuff.

270
00:13:01,566 --> 00:13:04,099
"I try my best
to avoid long conversations."

271
00:13:04,166 --> 00:13:05,599
This is my kind of stuff.

272
00:13:06,166 --> 00:13:10,833
"I will avoid going to certain locations.
I know a long talker is in that venue."

273
00:13:10,890 --> 00:13:14,026
"Is Pete there?
I can’t. I’m sorry, I’m not going to go."

274
00:13:14,099 --> 00:13:16,199
Like you know
you’re talking to a long talker

275
00:13:16,266 --> 00:13:18,133
when they say,
"Let me talk to you real quick."

276
00:13:18,199 --> 00:13:20,566
And no one says,
"Let me talk to you real slow."

277
00:13:22,066 --> 00:13:25,866
For some reason, long talkers,
they always need to rest something,

278
00:13:25,933 --> 00:13:27,866
whether it’s their hands on their knees.

279
00:13:27,933 --> 00:13:30,266
As soon as they say, "long story short,"
you’re in trouble.

280
00:13:36,066 --> 00:13:37,199
Me and Eddie Murphy talked.

281
00:13:37,266 --> 00:13:40,400
I’m like, "Eddie,
you’re a large reason why...

282
00:13:40,900 --> 00:13:42,133
I’m doing stand-up comedy."

283
00:13:42,199 --> 00:13:44,633
It’s never a white guy,
but be that as it may.

284
00:13:44,699 --> 00:13:46,933
What if I had said, "Dice was the reason.

285
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,333
I started because of Dice."

286
00:13:49,866 --> 00:13:53,433
But I’m talking to him and I’m like,
"Why don’t you want to do it?"

287
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:56,300
Because we’re talking,
and Eddie’s still funny.

288
00:13:56,402 --> 00:14:00,506
"We want to see it.
We want to see you, man. I want to see--"

289
00:14:00,573 --> 00:14:03,843
And what he said was,
"It’s like cold water.

290
00:14:03,933 --> 00:14:07,599
Because what I left from
is very hard to beat.

291
00:14:07,666 --> 00:14:09,800
The expectations are so high, and..."

292
00:14:09,866 --> 00:14:12,466
-I know.
-"...that’s what makes the water cold."

293
00:14:12,533 --> 00:14:15,633
When you see a great comedian
with a bunch of great stuff,

294
00:14:15,699 --> 00:14:21,099
you’re going, "Wow, I can’t believe
this guy crawled that far on his belly."

295
00:14:22,099 --> 00:14:24,066
‘Cause you know
that’s the only way he got there.

296
00:14:24,166 --> 00:14:26,233
I’ve had my lumps, in the beginning.

297
00:14:26,300 --> 00:14:27,599
I’ve had lumps, Jerry.

298
00:14:27,666 --> 00:14:31,633
Age 22, 24, I got a deal for $225,000.

299
00:14:33,166 --> 00:14:36,766
I just made it.

300
00:14:36,833 --> 00:14:39,266
I’m [bleep] rich.

301
00:14:39,333 --> 00:14:42,466
This is a real conversation
I had with my brother.

302
00:14:42,533 --> 00:14:46,166
"All right, first things first,
I’m going to get you and Mom a house.

303
00:14:46,233 --> 00:14:48,933
I’m going to go and get Dad,
square Dad away.

304
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,033
So that’s three houses,
let’s knock that out.

305
00:14:51,099 --> 00:14:52,666
Cars, who want what?

306
00:14:52,733 --> 00:14:55,333
Dad, you’ve been talking
about a truck, done.

307
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:58,366
I’m gonna go ahead out to LA,
probably find me something,

308
00:14:58,433 --> 00:14:59,833
but just know we all set."

309
00:15:00,599 --> 00:15:02,966
Instantly, money’s all gone.

310
00:15:03,033 --> 00:15:05,766
I’m on the phone
with my business manager at the time.

311
00:15:05,833 --> 00:15:08,466
"All right, Kevin, well we owe the IRS."

312
00:15:08,533 --> 00:15:10,300
You know, we hadn’t had the tax talk.

313
00:15:10,366 --> 00:15:11,733
We got to cut some deals.

314
00:15:12,166 --> 00:15:14,800
"Dad, we going to have
to sell that house."

315
00:15:15,466 --> 00:15:16,599
"Can’t do that, son."

316
00:15:16,666 --> 00:15:19,766
My dad had nine people
living in the house with him.

317
00:15:19,833 --> 00:15:23,333
But as comedians,
not a lot of us get a lot of chances.

318
00:15:23,400 --> 00:15:26,366
And I was in that grinding mode
for 13 years.

319
00:15:26,433 --> 00:15:27,966
-Oh, really?
-I was...

320
00:15:28,033 --> 00:15:30,900
I mean, Jerry, I’ve been doing comedy
since I was 18. I’m 35.

321
00:15:30,966 --> 00:15:34,233
My last four to five years
have been an upswing.

322
00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:36,666
You ever see that musical,
Little Shop of Horrors?

323
00:15:36,733 --> 00:15:38,800
-Yeah.
-I think that’s what TV is.

324
00:15:38,866 --> 00:15:41,033
One of us is going to get eaten here.

325
00:15:42,033 --> 00:15:43,633
-Who’s it gonna be?
-Who’s it gonna be?

326
00:15:43,699 --> 00:15:45,233
Either you’re going to eat me,

327
00:15:45,300 --> 00:15:46,833
-or I’m going to eat you.
-Yes.

328
00:15:46,900 --> 00:15:49,099
And I was at that point with the show,
and I thought,

329
00:15:49,166 --> 00:15:51,866
"I can’t believe I beat that box.

330
00:15:51,933 --> 00:15:53,199
But if I stay..."

331
00:15:53,266 --> 00:15:56,166
-It’s going to go bad.
-It’s going to go bad and it will eat me.

332
00:15:58,624 --> 00:16:01,627
By the way, this is probably
in the top two best conversations

333
00:16:01,699 --> 00:16:02,866
I’ve ever had in my life.

334
00:16:02,933 --> 00:16:07,500
The first one would be my dad explaining
to me why he had to do drugs.

335
00:16:07,866 --> 00:16:10,133
-Really?
-Hearing a man...

336
00:16:10,433 --> 00:16:12,199
"I had to... Listen.

337
00:16:12,666 --> 00:16:13,933
Everybody did it.

338
00:16:14,466 --> 00:16:15,966
You think I was by myself?

339
00:16:16,141 --> 00:16:19,445
Your aunt, your uncle, your..."

340
00:16:19,511 --> 00:16:22,047
Listen, that,
the best conversation I’ve ever had.

341
00:16:23,716 --> 00:16:25,184
[inaudible]

342
00:16:28,733 --> 00:16:31,300
I can’t think of one piece of knowledge
about LA

343
00:16:31,366 --> 00:16:33,333
that you need to have that you don’t have.

344
00:16:33,599 --> 00:16:36,199
You already understand
the concept of the monster.

345
00:16:36,266 --> 00:16:39,166
There’s not going to be a draw,
it’s not going to be a no decision.

346
00:16:39,233 --> 00:16:40,466
LA’s undefeated.

347
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:42,666
-Undefeated.
-It’s undefeated.

348
00:16:42,733 --> 00:16:45,633
No one has walked away with this town,

349
00:16:45,699 --> 00:16:48,066
-and LA’s going, "No, give it back."
-"No, come on, buddy."

350
00:16:48,133 --> 00:16:49,033
I love that line.

351
00:16:49,099 --> 00:16:50,900
-LA is undefeated.
-Undefeated, man.

352
00:16:53,779 --> 00:16:55,247
-[Kevin] There’s a sneaker shop?
-Yeah.

353
00:16:55,314 --> 00:16:57,216
-This is the best day ever, man.
-[Jerry laughs]

354
00:17:05,133 --> 00:17:07,566
All right, I’ll go for this one, too.
This is on me.

355
00:17:07,633 --> 00:17:09,399
-What, Jerry. No. Okay.
-Don’t even... No.

356
00:17:09,862 --> 00:17:11,063
[mouthing]

357
00:17:12,898 --> 00:17:15,067
This has just turned into
a weird male date.

358
00:17:18,866 --> 00:17:21,700
Come on, man. I'm gonna get that lean,
green dream.

359
00:17:21,766 --> 00:17:23,266
I'm gonna add hers to ours.

360
00:17:23,333 --> 00:17:24,500
That is so nice.

361
00:17:24,566 --> 00:17:26,500
-Thank you. You have a good day.
-Thank you.

362
00:17:27,266 --> 00:17:28,266
Cheers.

363
00:17:28,666 --> 00:17:31,233
Did you notice that she touched
your shoulder but not mine?

364
00:17:31,300 --> 00:17:32,866
I'm a very attractive guy, Jerry.

365
00:17:34,100 --> 00:17:36,300
You do have a likability about you.

366
00:17:36,355 --> 00:17:39,458
Yeah, but likability is nothing compared
to lovability.

367
00:17:39,525 --> 00:17:41,026
-You have lovability.
-Lovability is--

368
00:17:41,100 --> 00:17:43,433
Would you rather be smart or lovable?

369
00:17:43,500 --> 00:17:45,033
I’ve clearly made my choice.

370
00:17:45,233 --> 00:17:46,366
[both laughing]

371
00:17:47,266 --> 00:17:48,734
[woman squealing]

372
00:17:49,300 --> 00:17:50,866
I hate that high pitch.

373
00:17:50,933 --> 00:17:53,500
-What, the scream?
-It’s just the sound of dumbness to me.

374
00:18:00,612 --> 00:18:01,980
Oh, yeah.

375
00:18:02,333 --> 00:18:03,366
Oh, yeah.

376
00:18:03,649 --> 00:18:04,817
Yeah!

377
00:18:06,333 --> 00:18:07,266
Woo!

378
00:18:07,833 --> 00:18:09,166
All right!

379
00:18:09,233 --> 00:18:10,300
Go, Jerry, go!

380
00:18:10,366 --> 00:18:11,500
Yeah!

381
00:18:11,566 --> 00:18:14,466
This is amazing! This is great!

382
00:18:17,866 --> 00:18:19,233
[cell phone ringing]

383
00:18:20,433 --> 00:18:22,066
Babe, I can’t hear you.

384
00:18:22,133 --> 00:18:24,600
‘Cause I’m in a car with the top down.

385
00:18:25,137 --> 00:18:28,607
She said, "Don’t play with me,
you can hear me." That’s...

386
00:18:29,533 --> 00:18:31,966
I'll be at the hotel in a second.
I'll call right back, but--

387
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:35,200
All right. Bye.

388
00:18:36,600 --> 00:18:38,733
Something about women when you say,

389
00:18:38,800 --> 00:18:42,666
"I gotta go, I’m gonna call you back,"
they keep giving you more information.

390
00:18:45,800 --> 00:18:49,100
So my girl just found a dog.
I have a mini-Doberman Pinscher.

391
00:18:49,166 --> 00:18:53,966
She goes, "You know what would be good?
If we go get the bigger version of him.

392
00:18:54,033 --> 00:18:57,200
So we can have a mini-Doberman Pinscher,
and a big one."

393
00:18:57,266 --> 00:18:59,899
And I said, "Well, I think it’s kind
of mean to the mini one

394
00:18:59,938 --> 00:19:03,308
because he’s gonna get to look at
a bigger version of himself."

395
00:19:03,442 --> 00:19:05,511
-Like, "What did y'all do to me?"
-Until you...

396
00:19:05,711 --> 00:19:07,379
He’s gonna think we did something.

397
00:19:08,133 --> 00:19:09,833
[Kevin] "What did y'all do to me?!"

398
00:19:17,189 --> 00:19:18,357
I’ll put it in.

399
00:19:20,666 --> 00:19:22,266
Are you coming to Vegas soon?

400
00:19:22,333 --> 00:19:24,233
I was just there, two weeks ago,
you missed it.

401
00:19:24,300 --> 00:19:26,133
-Are you coming back?
-Always. Yeah.

402
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:27,800
-The day after Christmas.
-[indistinct]

403
00:19:27,900 --> 00:19:29,601
-Is that what I’m worth?
-[laughs]

